WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.900
[upbeat music]

2
00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.780
Welcome. In this video lesson, we're
diving into a fundamental concept,

3
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:10.320
heterogeneous versus
homogeneous disaggregation.

4
00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:14.740
These approaches define how
large system on chips, or SoCs,

5
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.200
are broken down
and reassembled using chiplets.

6
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:22.260
As we push the limits of monolithic SoCs,

7
00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.440
disaggregation emerges
as a powerful solution.

8
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:30.140
It allows smaller modular chiplets
to be assembled into a single package,

9
00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:34.800
enhancing yield, cost efficiency,
flexibility, and crucially,

10
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:38.420
improving SWaP, size, weight, and power.

11
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.880
Homogeneous disaggregation
means breaking down a large

12
00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.620
SoC into smaller chiplets
that use the same process node.

13
00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.540
Each chiplet retains the same function
as part of the original design,

14
00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.600
optimizing silicon utilization, and
improving yield due to smaller die sizes.

15
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.860
Heterogeneous disaggregation, on the other
hand, takes this concept further.

16
00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.190
Instead of keeping everything
in the same process node,

17
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.500
we optimize functional
blocks into different nodes.

18
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.110
This allows, for example, logic
to be built in an advanced

19
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.060
node while analog and I/O remain
on mature, cost-effective nodes.

20
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.140
A real-world example of heterogeneous
integration is high bandwidth memory,

21
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.630
or HBM, which is built in an advanced
DRAM process node and stacks memory

22
00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.380
on top of a base logic die
using 3D integration techniques.

23
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:37.070
The key advantages of homogeneous
disaggregation include cost

24
00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.940
savings due to higher yield
or building a smaller unit die,

25
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.880
such as a CPU core, that can be repeated
several times to scale performance.

26
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.940
Heterogeneous disaggregation,
meanwhile, unlocks greater efficiency

27
00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.600
by tailoring each function
to the most suitable process node.

28
00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:59.500
As chips in older process nodes are
faster to design and cheaper to build,

29
00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:02.740
this means lower cost
and faster time to market.

30
00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:07.660
Additionally, an optimized process
node can also improve performance.

31
00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:11.000
In summary, both approaches
serve different needs.

32
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:14.860
Homogeneous disaggregation is
about efficiency and yield,

33
00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:18.650
while heterogeneous disaggregation
maximizes performance

34
00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.040
and cost savings by integrating
optimized functional blocks.

35
00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:26.150
This is just the beginning of what's
possible with chiplet architectures.

36
00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.690
Later in this course, we'll explore
how package-level aggregation

37
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.920
compares to PCB-based integration.

38
00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:37.460
[upbeat music]

